<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Engelbert II of Berg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1185–<a href="/years/1225">1225</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Saint Engelbert, Engelbert I of Cologne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Engelbert II of Berg was Count of Berg and, from 1216, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Emperor Frederick II made him regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and guardian of his son Henry (VII) in 1220. A forceful ruler who curbed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> nobles, he was murdered in 1225 near Gevelsberg by men of his kinsman Frederick of Isenberg, and he was later venerated as a saint and martyr.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/engelbert-ii-of-berg.jpg" alt="Engelbert II of Berg" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Gawain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ReliquienbuesteEngelbertvonKoeln.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
